## Class outline

1. Homework presentations guided by the <a href={critiqueWheelHref}>Wheel of Feedback</a>
2. Break
3. Presentation on news writing
4. Story dissection exercise
5. New homework assignments for next week

## Story dissection exercise

Break into groups of 2 or 3 and analyze one of the following stories.

### Stories

- Gothamist: [Complaints of parking placard abuse by NYC employees double in a year, 311 data shows](https://gothamist.com/news/complaints-of-parking-placard-abuse-by-nyc-employees-double-in-a-year-311-data-shows)
- The City Reporter: [Newbie Cops Get Thrown at Problem Areas Leading to Rookie Mistakes and Attrition](https://www.thecity.nyc/2023/05/01/newbie-cops-get-thrown-at-problem-areas/)
- Streetsblog: [Data Analysis: Super Speeders and Red Light Violators Are Less Likely to Get NYPD Tickets](https://nyc.streetsblog.org/2026/01/30/data-analysis-super-speeders-and-red-light-violators-are-less-likely-to-get-nypd-tickets)
- Hell Gate: [The NYPD's Watchdog Whitewashed More Than 10,000 Misconduct Allegations in Public Data, New Evidence Shows](https://hellgatenyc.com/ccrb-whitewashed-more-than-10000-allegations/)
- City Limits: [How NYC Closed Tens of Thousands of 'No Heat' Complaints Without Conducting an Inspection](https://citylimits.org/how-nyc-closed-tens-of-thousands-of-no-heat-complaints-without-conducting-an-inspection/)

### Questions

1. Can you find the four elements in the story? Are any missing?
2. What's the most important number? Where does it appear?
3. Can you find any numbers that could be cut?

## Homework

### Task 1: Develop a simple finding

Return to a data set you've been analyzing in previous weeks. Develop a simple finding you can back up with a number you calculated yourself. It doesn't have to be earth-shattering news but it should be an answer to a question that you can clearly explain.

### Task 2: Draft your top

Write the top of a news story, about 100–150 words, for a general reader. Use a direct lead, not a delayed lead. Open with the news itself, stated plainly, in the first sentence. Use numbers sparingly. Spell out what you've found without a data dump. Your top must hit all four fundamentals: the lead, the nut, the findings and the caveats.

### Task 3: Prepare to share

Next week, everyone will read their top aloud. Be ready to share the spreadsheet work or other calculation behind your finding.
